    "allText": "===ARTICLE 0===\nShams\n@ShamsAmin56\nThe Sportage Turbo Tuk-Tuk is ready to dominate Tokyo Drift.\n\nMade with @Flovaai_Japan @Flovaai \n\nPrompt and Media Below - Prompt in two parts\n#Flovaai #Flovacpp\n00:39\n10:13 AM · Sep 17, 2026\n·\n464\nViews\n7\n2\n21\n\n===ARTICLE 1===\nShams\n@ShamsAmin56\n4h\nZED — Driver Reference: exact character design, face, hairstyle, goggles, black/neon-lime outfit, proportions and accessories.\n\nNEON RIDER — TukTuk Reference: exact neon-lime/black racing TukTuk, wide body kit, exposed turbo/engine, roll cage, carbon panels, oversized rear wing and wheels.\n\nPART 1 — 0:00–0:30 — “COFFEE TO CHAOS”\n\nCreate a 30-second stylized 3D animated cinematic set in Japan. Premium feature-animation character quality mixed with high-energy automotive cinematography. Maintain exact reference consistency throughout. Zed and NEON RIDER must never change design, proportions, clothing, colors, body kit, wheels, wing, roll cage, lights or mechanical components. Japanese urban environment, warm late-afternoon transitioning toward neon evening. Funky energetic soundtrack begins subtly in the café and builds into Japanese street-racing energy. Detailed cloth physics, hair motion, reflections, tire deformation, suspension movement, dust, exhaust smoke and environmental particles.\n\n0:00–0:04 — CAFÉ INTRO / CLOSE-UP #1–2\nInterior of a small stylish Japanese roadside café. Establishing shot through the window: Japanese street signs, bicycles and traffic outside. Camera slowly dollies toward ZED, sitting casually at a tiny table finishing his coffee. CLOSE-UP #1: macro shot of Zed's mischievous eyes beneath his goggles. He glances outside and sees something that makes him grin. Rack focus from his face toward the blurred neon-green shape of NEON RIDER outside.\n\nCLOSE-UP #2: coffee cup on table. Zed flicks the cup with one finger and performs an absurdly smooth 360° cup spin. Camera orbits slightly with it. Tiny coffee droplets wobble around the rim but never spill. Zed catches the cup perfectly and raises one eyebrow.\n0:04–0:08 — KEYS / COMEDY EXIT / CLOSE-UP #3\nExtreme macro CLOSE-UP #3: TukTuk keys lying beside the cup. Zed slaps his palm down—keys briefly jump upward. He catches them without looking. Metallic CLINK. He gets up with exaggerated swagger.\n\nLow-angle tracking shot follows his chunky shoes toward the café door. Instead of using his hand, Zed turns backward and bumps the door open with his hip, then does a tiny victorious shoulder shimmy. Keep the humor physical and expressive, not cartoonishly weightless.\n\n0:08–0:12 — HERO REVEAL\nAs the door swings open, transition into a dramatic low ground-level reveal of NEON RIDER parked outside. Camera starts at the front tire and performs a fast controlled arc around the TukTuk: aggressive front splitter → neon-lime body → exposed side mechanics → turbo → roll cage → giant rear wing.\nDust motes float through sunlight. Metallic surfaces catch Japanese signage reflections.\n\nZed walks into frame, casually spinning the keys around one finger.\n\n0:12–0:16 — GETTING IN / CLOSE-UP #4–5\nSide profile camera. Zed hops into NEON RIDER with one exaggerated athletic movement and almost hits his head on the roll cage—he ducks at the last instant and gives the cage an annoyed look.\nCLOSE-UP #4: hand grabs the steering control.\n\nCLOSE-UP #5: fingers insert the key into the ignition.\n\nHold clearly on every mechanical action.\n\nCLICK.\nZed turns the key.\n\n0:16–0:21 — ENGINE START / CLOSE-UP #6–8\nCLOSE-UP #6: instrument cluster completely dark.\n\nKey turns.\n\nDashboard wakes sequentially—speedometer illumination sweeps across, warning lights pulse, tachometer needle performs a rapid startup sweep.\n\nVRR—VRR—BRAAAP!\n\nCLOSE-UP #7: exposed engine violently shudders to life. Belts, pulleys and turbo components vibrate realistically.\n\nCLOSE-UP #8: rear muffler. One deep ignition pulse blasts a compact cloud of gray-white exhaust smoke toward camera. Condensation particles and heat distortion visible.\nCut to Zed's face. His grin widens.\n\n0:21–0:25 — FIRST GEAR\nMacro mechanical shot: Zed firmly selects FIRST GEAR.\n\nCLACK.\n\nCut underneath the TukTuk: drivetrain engages.\n\nRear suspension squats slightly.\n\nRear tires rotate a fraction.\n\nSide camera slowly pushes toward the rear wheel while engine RPM climbs.\n\n0:25–0:30 — LAUNCH\nZed gives the throttle a playful double tap.\nBRAAP—BRAAAAAAP!\n\nLow rear three-quarter shot: rear tires suddenly spin. Dense tire smoke curls around the wide wheels. Fine road dust and tiny rubber particles kick backward.\n\nNEON RIDER launches down the Japanese street.\n\nCamera rapidly transitions between front wheel tracking → side-mounted wheel shot → rear chase camera → extremely low front three-quarter tracking shot.\n\nThe TukTuk accelerates toward the first corner as pedestrians turn toward the sound.\n\nAt 0:29, camera moves alongside Zed. He looks toward the approaching turn with a ridiculous confident grin.\n\nAt 0:30, Zed snaps the steering input and NEON RIDER begins rotating into its first drift.\nCUT PART 1 WHILE THE DRIFT IS ALREADY IN MOTION.\nMade with AI\n1\n115\n\n===ARTICLE 2===\nShams\n@ShamsAmin56\n4h\nPART 2 — 0:30–1:00 — ZED × NEON RIDER: TOKYO DRIFT CHAOS\n\nCreate a 30-second premium 3D animated automotive cinematic as the direct continuation of Part 1. Use ZED as the exact character reference and NEON RIDER as the exact TukTuk reference. Push the cinematography much further: extreme lens placement, foreground wipes, whip transitions, speed ramps, chase cameras, wheel-level shots, overhead rotations, reflection shots and dramatic parallax. Every camera move must reveal either Zed's personality, the TukTuk's mechanical detail, or the drift physics.\n\n0:30–0:34 — DRIFT THROUGH THE CAMERA\n\nContinue the exact moving camera from Part 1 for the first second.\n\nNEON RIDER's rear swings outward as Zed counter-steers hard.\n\nCamera suddenly drops toward asphalt level and races backward only centimeters ahead of the front wheel, using an ultra-wide 18mm perspective. The front wheel counter-steers toward camera while the TukTuk slides diagonally across the frame.\n\nWHIP beneath the side bodywork into an extreme close tracking shot of the spinning rear wheel.\n\nRubber flexes.\n\nSmoke explodes around lens.\n\nInstead of cutting away, let the camera become completely swallowed by tire smoke.\n\nUse the smoke itself as a natural transition.\n\nCamera BURSTS OUT of the smoke behind NEON RIDER just as traction returns.\n\nBRAAAAAAP!\n\nTurbo spools.\n\nThe TukTuk fires forward.\n\n0:34–0:38 — ZED POV → REFLECTION → CHASE\n\nStart with an extreme close-up of Zed's eye reflected inside his green goggles.\n\nIn the curved goggle reflection we see the next Japanese corner rushing toward him.\n\nRack focus onto his mischievous grin.\n\nSMASH CUT TO FIRST-PERSON DRIVER POV.\n\nHands working the steering.\n\nDashboard shaking.\n\nJapanese storefronts rush past both sides.\n\nSpeedometer climbing.\n\nZed snaps the gear.\n\nCLACK!\n\nCamera instantly transitions through the open side of the TukTuk into a floating exterior side shot, only centimeters from Zed.\n\nHe glances directly toward camera and gives a stupidly confident eyebrow raise.\n\nThe camera falls backward toward the turbo.\n\nWHOOSH → TURBO CLOSE-UP → EXHAUST BURST → REAR CHASE.\n\n0:38–0:42 — CROWD STREET / CAMERA GAUNTLET\n\nNEON RIDER rockets into a packed Japanese street section with spectators safely behind barriers.\n\nDo NOT use a conventional wide shot.\n\nCamera begins inside the crowd, looking between two spectators.\n\nNEON RIDER appears in the distance.\n\nIt approaches incredibly fast.\n\nAt the last instant—\n\nWHIP PAN!\n\nThe TukTuk blasts past camera.\n\nImmediately match-cut to a camera mounted almost directly beside the rear wheel.\n\nThen cut to a long telephoto shot through foreground lanterns, compressing Zed, the TukTuk and cheering crowd into layers.\n\nNEON RIDER initiates another drift.\n\nCamera travels parallel to the TukTuk while facing directly through the open cabin, showing Zed fighting the steering while the entire Japanese background moves sideways behind him.\n\nZed waves casually at one spectator—\n\nthen realizes the corner tightens.\n\nHis eyes suddenly widen.\n\n“OH—!”\n\nHe grabs the controls with both hands.\n\n0:42–0:46 — S-BEND / ORBITING DRIFT SHOT\n\nZed aggressively flicks left.\n\nLEFT DRIFT.\n\nInstead of cutting immediately, camera performs a fast 270° orbit around the moving TukTuk while the vehicle itself continues drifting forward.\n\nBegin at rear three-quarter → sweep beside the exposed turbo → pass Zed through the open cabin → arrive at front three-quarter.\n\nRear tires pour smoke.\n\nZed transitions instantly—\n\nLEFT → RIGHT.\n\nApply a sharp speed ramp into 40% slow motion.\n\nCamera becomes almost stationary at road level as NEON RIDER slides sideways toward lens.\n\nFront splitter passes extremely close.\n\nSCRAAAAAPE!\n\nCarbon touches asphalt.\n\nSparks streak across the foreground in slow motion.\n\nIndividual rubber particles and dust float through the light.\n\nAs the rear wheel passes camera—\n\nFULL SPEED.\n\nThe wheel fills the entire frame and becomes a natural circular wipe transition.\n\n0:46–0:52 — THE ROUNDABOUT MONEY SHOT\n\nReveal a Japanese roundabout using a high-speed drone-style camera descending backward toward NEON RIDER.\n\nZed sees the roundabout.\n\nExtreme close-up.\n\nEyebrow rises.\n\nTiny grin.\n\nHe jerks the steering.\n\nNEON RIDER enters SIDEWAYS.\n\nCamera initially follows from only a few meters above the rear wing.\n\nAs the TukTuk begins circling, camera rapidly climbs while simultaneously rotating until it becomes a perfect 90° TOP-DOWN view centered over the roundabout.\n\nNow perform the signature shot:\n\nNEON RIDER DRIFTS EXACTLY ONE COMPLETE LAP AROUND THE ROUNDABOUT.\n\nNot a stationary donut.\n\nThe TukTuk physically travels the entire circumference while maintaining a controlled drift.\n\nThe overhead camera slowly rotates opposite the TukTuk's direction, creating dramatic counter-motion.\n\nNeon-lime vehicle.\n\nDark asphalt.\n\nWhite smoke.\n\nThe rear tires gradually paint a perfect circular smoke ring around the center island.\n\nCrowds form an outer visual ring.\n\nAt approximately 75% of the lap, begin dropping the camera altitude.\n\nThe TukTuk completes EXACTLY ONE ROUND.\n\nZed finds the exit.\n\nNEON RIDER shoots out.\n\nInstead of cutting—\n\nCAMERA DIVES FROM THE SKY DIRECTLY BEHIND THE REAR WING, transitioning from top-down into a high-speed chase shot.\n\n0:52–0:56 — NEON TUNNEL RUN\n\nCamera chases only centimeters above the asphalt.\n\nNEON RIDER fills the center frame.\n\nJapanese lights stretch into reflections across its carbon bodywork.\n\nMove camera rapidly toward the TukTuk—\n\nUNDER THE REAR WING.\n\nPass visually alongside the exposed engine.\n\nTurbo glowing with heat.\n\nExhaust vibrating.\n\nCut inside cabin.\n\nExtreme close-up of Zed's hand changing gear.\n\nCLACK.\n\nCut directly to turbo.\n\nPSHHHT!\n\nCut directly to muffler.\n\nBRAAAP!\n\nThen use the exhaust smoke as another transition into a dramatic front-facing reverse chase camera.\n\nNEON RIDER charges toward camera.\n\nZed sees the final corner.\n\nHis smile disappears.\n\nSerious face.\n\nHalf-second pause.\n\nThen an enormous grin.\n\n0:56–0:59 — FINAL HERO DRIFT\n\nZed throws the TukTuk sideways.\n\nFULL-POWER FINAL DRIFT.\n\nBegin with an ultra-low 85mm front three-quarter shot.\n\nAs NEON RIDER approaches sideways, camera rapidly changes into a wide 24mm moving orbit without losing the vehicle.\n\nCamera rotates around the TukTuk:\n\nFRONT → OPEN CABIN / ZED → TURBO → REAR WING → REAR THREE-QUARTER.\n\nSmoke curls around camera.\n\nFor approximately 0.7 seconds, enter dramatic slow motion.\n\nZed visible through the smoke.\n\nFront wheel at full counter-steer.\n\nSuspension loaded.\n\nRear tire spinning.\n\nSparks underneath.\n\nTurbo vibrating.\n\nZed laughing.\n\nThen—\n\nSPEED RAMP.\n\nNEON RIDER snaps straight.\n\nZed slams the brake.\n\nThe TukTuk slides precisely sideways into a parking position beside a tiny Japanese coffee stand.\n\nSTOP.\n\nCamera continues moving after the TukTuk stops, creating dramatic parallax before smoothly swinging around to Zed.\n\n0:59–1:00 — COMEDY BUTTON\n\nMusic suddenly cuts.\n\nSmoke slowly crosses frame.\n\nEngine:\n\nrumble... rumble...\n\nWithout leaving his seat, Zed casually reaches toward the coffee stand.\n\nThe confused worker hands him a tiny coffee.\n\nExtreme close-up.\n\nZed takes one sophisticated sip.\n\nBehind him—\n\nBRAAAAAAAP!\n\nThe muffler blasts a tiny smoke cloud.\n\nZed freezes mid-sip.\n\nHis eyes slowly slide sideways.\n\nCamera performs a fast punch-in on his offended expression.\n\nTiny engine response:\n\n“brap.”\n\nZed slowly grins.\n\nSMASH CUT TO BLACK ON THE FINAL MUSIC HIT.\n\nCINEMATOGRAPHY + CONSISTENCY LOCK\n\nTreat ZED and NEON RIDER as immutable assets. No character redesign, wardrobe changes, vehicle modifications, wheel changes, altered body kit, changed wing, changed roll cage or color shifts. Keep the same Japanese environment, time of day and cinematic grade established in Part 1.\n\nAvoid repetitive generic front/side/rear shots. Favor 18–24mm extreme tracking, 50–85mm compressed hero shots, asphalt-level cameras, wheel proximity shots, smoke wipes, foreground occlusion, reflection shots, cabin POV, moving orbits, drone dives, parallax and motivated speed ramps.\n\nCamera movement must remain physically readable: no teleporting cameras, random cuts or impossible orientation changes. Use objects such as smoke, spinning wheels, passing signs, crowds, pillars and body panels as natural wipes between shots.\n\nPrioritize visible automotive physics: counter-steering, chassis roll, suspension compression/rebound, tire deformation, wheelspin, traction recovery, turbo vibration, exhaust pressure, heat distortion, rubber debris, dust, sparks and volumetric tire smoke.\n\nThe result should feel like one uninterrupted 60-second 3D animated Japanese street-racing film—not two separate generated clips.\nMade with AI\n84\n\n===ARTICLE 3===\nReNature🦋\n@Natursee\n3h\nVery successful work i like that\n1\n2\n23",
